  "sections": [
    {
      "id": "pakistan-afghanistan-folk-traditional-voices-i",
      "title": "Pakistan & Afghanistan Folk — Traditional Voices I",
      "level": 1,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "**Track:** South Asian Traditions | **Region:** south-northwest | **Tier:** 2\n**Prerequisites:** regions-south-northwest-01-overview\n**Tags:** south, northwest, south-asia, traditional, voices"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "overview",
      "title": "Overview",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "rubab, sitar, voice\n\nPakistan & Afghanistan Folk belongs to the **South Asian Traditions** curriculum. Nusrat Fateh Ali Khan\n\nThis lesson develops listening precision for **rubab, sitar, voice** — connecting repertoire, instruments, dance, and the institutions that transmit music today.\n\n![Traditional musicians](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Fiddle.jpg?width=640)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "compared-with-hindustani-tradition",
      "title": "Compared with Hindustani tradition",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "**Hindustani Music** (`regions-south-hindustani-02-instruments-voices-i`) covers the same lesson slot from the anchor perspective. This lesson stresses: **rubab, sitar, voice**.\n\n| Topic | Hindustani tradition anchor | Pakistan & Afghanistan Folk (this lesson) |\n|-------|------------------------------|-------------------------------------|\n| Focus | sitar, sarod, voice… | rubab, sitar, voice… |\n\n**Parallel anchor lesson:** `regions-south-hindustani-02-instruments-voices-i`\n**Secondary comparison (Ireland):** `regions-celtic-ireland-02-instruments-voices-i`"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "core-material",
      "title": "Core material",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "rubab, sitar, voice\n\nStudy **pakistan & afghanistan folk** through listening first. Wikipedia's article on [Music of Pakistan](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Music_of_Pakistan) orients geography and history; recordings prove how musicians phrase ornaments, tempo, and ensemble balance in real rooms."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "regional-accent-and-social-context",
      "title": "Regional accent and social context",
      "level": 3,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Tradition lives in **community practice** — festivals, dances, teaching networks, and archives — not only on concert stages. For **South Asian Traditions**, ask who leads (voice, fiddle, pipes, percussion), whether dance drives repertoire, and how revival institutions shaped what you hear on modern recordings."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "instruments-and-voices-in-this-lesson",
      "title": "Instruments and voices in this lesson",
      "level": 3,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Lead voices: **rubab, sitar, voice**. Ensemble layer: **tabla, ensemble**. Compare timbre and role with the anchor unit before assuming techniques transfer unchanged."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "forms-dance-and-rhythm",
      "title": "Forms, dance, and rhythm",
      "level": 3,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Named forms include **ghazal, regional folk**. Dance context: **social dance**. Metre and foot-tap feel are not decorative — they explain why musicians accent certain beats and phrase repeats."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "history-transmission-and-today",
      "title": "History, transmission, and today",
      "level": 3,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Historical threads: **cross-border repertoire**. Contemporary scenes: **festivals**. Tune repertoire anchor: **Regional listening**. Follow lesson playlists and reading lists for recorded examples."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "listening-habits",
      "title": "Listening habits",
      "level": 3,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "1. Queue one field or archive recording and one contemporary ensemble track.\n2. Log metre, lead instrument, ornament, and social context in four columns.\n3. Return to the parallel anchor lesson if comparison feels abstract.\n4. Avoid generic world-music compilations when building ear training."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "study-facts",
      "title": "Study facts",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "- rubab, sitar, voice\n- Lead instruments: rubab, sitar, voice\n- Ensemble: tabla, ensemble\n- Forms: ghazal, regional folk\n- Dance: social dance\n- History: cross-border repertoire\n- Scenes: festivals\n- Repertoire: Regional listening\n- Compared with Hindustani tradition: regional accent and social context differ.\n- Revival movements and media shaped twentieth-century practice.\n- Dance rhythm and tune metre are linked in this tradition."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "key-points",
      "title": "Key points",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "- Pakistan & Afghanistan Folk is a living tradition with regional accent.\n- Focus: rubab, sitar, voice.\n- Compared with Hindustani tradition: shared heritage, distinct emphasis.\n- Forms: ghazal, regional folk.\n- Dance: social dance.\n- Transmission: cross-border repertoire."
        }
      ]
    },
